==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈhʌŋki/|lang=en}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{suffix|hunk|y|lang=en}}.

====Adjective====
{{en-adj|er}}

# {{lb|en|slang}} Exhibiting strong, masculine beauty.
# Shaped like a [[hunk]], or piece; [[chunky]].
# {{lb|en|US|slang}} All right; in good condition.
# {{lb|en|US|slang}} [[even]]; [[square]]; on equal footing ''with''
#* Stephen Crane
#*: {{...}} he dropped like a brick into the firing line and began to shoot; began to get "'''hunky'''" with all those people who had been plugging at him.

=====Related terms=====
* {{l|en|hunk}}

===Etymology 2===
From the older {{m|en|hunk}}, probably alteration of {{m|en|Hungarian}}. Compare {{m|en|bohunk}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|hunkie}}
* {{l|en|hunkey}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun|hunkies}}

# {{lb|en|North America|slang|ethnic slur}} A person of [[Hungarian]] or [[Slavic]], especially [[Ruthenia]]n, descent.
#* {{quote-text|year=2009|author=Victor Bockris|title=Warhol: The Biography|page=20|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=NRmjRcSYXlgC&lpg=PA20&dq=hunkies&hl=ru&pg=PA20#v=onepage&q=hunkies&f=false|passage=Like blacks, who were the only ethnic group below them on the social scale, Eastern Europeans, contemptuously labelled ‘'''hunkies'''’, were dismissed as incapable and untrustworthy.}}

